\v 31 \wj Then¦48506 it so¦48515 happened¦48518 that a¦48507 priest¦48507 came¦48509 down¦48509 that¦48514 same road¦48513, but when¦48515 he saw¦48516 him¦48517 he continued past on the other side of the road.\wj*
\v 32 \wj Similarly¦48519, a priestly worker (a Levite¦48522) arrived¦48527 at that place¦48526 and¦48528 when he saw¦48529 him he too passed¦48531 by¦48531 on the other side.\wj*
\v 33 \wj Then a¦48532 man from¦48532 Shomron¦48532 \+add (Samaria)\+add*\wj*\f + \fr 10:33 \ft The Judeans and the Samaritans despised each other and disagreed on many vital issues.\f* \wj who was on a trip came¦48536 across him¦48538, and¦48539 when he saw¦48540 him he¦48542 felt¦48542 \+add ≈sorry¦48542\+add* for him\wj*\x + \xo 10:33-34: \xt 2Ch 28:15.\x*
\v 34 \wj and¦48543 went up¦48545 to him and bandaged¦48545 his¦48548 wounds¦48547, pouring¦48549 olive¦48551 oil¦48551 and¦48552 wine¦48553 on them, then¦48556 put him¦48557 on¦48559 his¦48560 own¦48561 animal and took¦48563 him¦48564 to¦48565 a roadhouse and¦48567 looked after him¦48569.\wj*
\v 35 \wj The next¦48574 day¦48572 he¦48578 took¦48588 out¦48577 two¦48579 coins¦48580 and gave¦48578 them to¦48583 their host¦48584, instructing, ‘Look after this guy and if it costs more than this, I'll¦48603 reimburse you¦48595 on¦48598 my way¦48599 back¦48603.’\wj*
